latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Identify the Masked Area:\n   - Locate all cells with value 2 (red) in the input grid.\n\n2. Analyze Global Patterns and Symmetry:\n   - Determine the type of symmetry present (rotational, reflectional, or both).\n   - Identify recurring patterns in non-masked areas.\n\n3. Create a Symmetry Mapping:\n   - For each masked cell, identify its symmetric counterparts in the grid.\n   - This mapping will be used to ensure global symmetry is maintained.\n\n4. Perform Initial Fill:\n   - For each masked cell, examine its non-masked neighbors and symmetric counterparts.\n   - Fill the cell with the most common color among these, considering the context of surrounding patterns.\n\n5. Enforce Global Symmetry:\n   - Use the symmetry mapping to ensure that symmetric cells have the same color.\n   - Adjust colors if necessary to maintain rotational and/or reflectional symmetry.\n\n6. Ensure Pattern Continuity:\n   - Analyze patterns in non-masked areas adjacent to the masked region.\n   - Extend these patterns into the masked area, adjusting as needed to maintain symmetry.\n\n7. Balance Color Distribution:\n   - Compare the color distribution in the filled area to the rest of the grid.\n   - Make adjustments to ensure the filled area's color distribution is consistent with the overall grid.\n\n8. Perform Final Symmetry and Coherence Check:\n   - Do a final pass to ensure perfect symmetry is maintained.\n   - Check that the filled area integrates seamlessly with the surrounding patterns.\n\n9. Return the Completed Grid:\n   - Once all checks are passed, return the grid with the reconstructed pattern.\n\nThis plan addresses the key aspects we identified in our reflection:\n- It maintains global symmetry (steps 3, 5, and 8).\n- It continues patterns from non-masked areas (steps 4 and 6).\n- It adapts the filling strategy based on the specific characteristics of each grid (steps 2, 4, and 6).\n- It preserves consistency in color distribution (step 7).\n- It takes into account the broader context of the entire grid (steps 2, 6, and 8).\n\nBy following this plan, we should be able to create an algorithm that can handle a wide variety of grid patterns and masked area configurations, addressing all the cases we've seen in the examples."
